 { Wilhelm Steinitz A sacrifice is best refuted by accepting it.985. Wilhelm Steinitz – Philipp Meitner, Vienna 1859 } { _ } 1. { -KEY- } e6 $1 fxe6 ( 1... dxe6 2. Bb5 ( { The move order, } 1... fxe6 1. Qd3 Nac6 3. Bxc7 { transposes. } ) 
 ( 1... fxe6 1. Bxc7 Nac6 3. Qd3 f6 { when White has a winning position due to the black king, even though it’s not over yet. } ) 
c6 ( 2... Nac6 3. Nf5 $1 { and as the e7-knight is overloaded, White will soon win the knight on c6 (or the rook on a8) . } ) 
3. Bc7 ( { Another winning line is, } 2... fxe6 2. Bd6 cxb5 4. Bxe7 Kxe7 5. Nf5 { taking the queen. } ) 
Bd7 4. Bxa5 Qg4 $1 ( { Material is equal after } 4... cxb5 { but White wins with the double threat } 5. Qxb7 ( { Or } 4... fxe6 4. Rxd7 Kxd7 6. Qxb7 ) 
 ) 
5. Qd3 $1 { The only winning move; Black doesn’t get time to take on h4 for free. } Nd5 6. Re4 $1 { White is a piece up. } ) 
 ( 1... Nxc4 { ✔ and White wins with three moves, of which you should have seen one: } ) 
 ( { A. } 1... fxe6 1. Nf5 { and if the queen moves, White takes on f7/d7 and e7. } ) 
 ( { B. } 1... fxe6 1. exd7 Bxd7 3. Qxb7 { +- is complicated. } ) 
 ( { C. Easiest is } 1... fxe6 1. exf7 Qxf7 3. Nf5 { +– when Black can’t defend e7 . } ) 
2. Qxh5+ { ✔ and White picks up the knight on a5. } 
 1-0

