 { 999. Alexander Alekhine – Max Euwe, Netherlands (16) 1937 Finding the tactical theme is not difficult, however it’s not easy to decide when to stop calculating and instead evaluate the position. } 1. { -KEY- } Rxd7 $1 Bxd7 { Other recaptures give away a second minor piece. } 2. Ng5 Qb8 ( { Not } 2... Bc6 $2 3. Bxc6 Qxc6 4. Nce4 { ✔ hitting the queen and threatening mate on h7 . } ) 
3. Nxh7 $1 ( { ✔ Instead the game continued } 3. Bxa8 $5 Qxa8 ( { If } 3. Nxh7 h6 { then White wins a pawn after retreating the bishop from a8 . } ) 
4. Nxh7 { White is a pawn up, however the bishop pair gives Black compensation for half of it after } Rc8 { (½–½, 65 moves) (.) } ) 
 { The reason it is stronger to delay the capture on a8 is: } Rc8 ( { Or } 3... Nxh7 4. Qxe7 { wins the exchange back with an extra pawn. } ) 
4. Ng5 ( { Black cannot play } 4. Ng5 e5 { (planning ...Bf5) due to } 5. Nd5 { – a move that would not be possible after the capture on a8 . } ) 
Bc6 { This is the only move that tries to keep the rook. Now follows: } 5. Nce4 $1 Bxe4 6. Rxc8+ Qxc8 7. Bxe4 Rb8 8. Bd4 $1 ( { White’s beautiful threat is } 8. Bd4 -- 9. Bh7 Kf8 10. Bg6 $3 { when the bishop on d4 supports the queen after } fxg6 11. Qh8 Ng8 12. Bxg7 { with a decisive attack. } ) 
 { Black's best defense is: } Qc1+ 9. Kg2 Rd8 $1 10. Bh7+ Kf8 11. Bg6 Rxd4 $1 { The forced moves end after: } 12. Qh8+ Ng8 13. Nh7+ $1 Ke8 14. Qxg8+ Kd7 15. Qxf7 { Two pawns, an ongoing attack and opposite-coloured bishops give White a clear advantage. But seeing this far is neither realistic, nor woodpecking. Full points for 3 Nxh7!. } 
